Sessions 2-3: for each hand-written CUDA kernel (RMSNorm fwd/bwd, RoPE, fused softmax-CE, GEMM epilogue), run hipify-perl into kernels/hip/, then apply the wave64 fixes:
- 64-bit masks;
- warpSize=64 assumptions;
- __launch_bounds__ and amdgpu_waves_per_eu;
- LDS of at most 64 KiB;
- for hot reductions, use DPP builtins (__builtin_amdgcn_update_dpp / mov_dpp) instead of __shfl_xor, which lowers to ds_bpermute.
Compile device-only with CCE clang for gfx908/gfx90a/gfx942, using the fake ROCm path recipe in .../hipify_test/fake_rocm. Keep the .s and .hsaco files next to the sm_90 SASS for side-by-side tables.

Sessions 3-4: build and test a 1-2 GPU-hour 'AMD smoke pack' so the first hardware window produces data immediately:
- env check: rocminfo, torch.version.hip, torch.cuda.get_device_properties(0).gcnArchName, device name;
- correctness: torch.testing.assert_close against eager on the AMD device for every kernel;
- microbenchmarks timed by rocprofv3 --kernel-trace per-kernel times, not Python wall clock;
- rocprof-compute on 2 hot kernels with roofline;
- nanochat reduced config for 100 steps plus scripts/infer_bench.py, with NANOCHAT_ATTN_IMPL=sdpa and HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1;
- results written to results/amd_<arch>.json, using the same seeds and configs as the GH200 baseline.

